faq:
  - q: "How is this different from a one-day AI workshop?"
    a: "A workshop teaches awareness; three months changes behaviour. Support workers who sat through a workshop are back to writing notes the old way within a fortnight, because nobody built the habit on real shifts. Our program runs across three months on your own documentation, so by the end it is simply how the work gets done — and ongoing support keeps it current after that."
  - q: "Does AI-assisted documentation meet NDIS Commission expectations?"
    a: "The Commission's concern is that records are accurate, contemporaneous and attributable to the worker — not which tool produced the first draft. A verified, signed note written on the day is stronger evidence than one reconstructed from memory a week later. We train the verification step explicitly, and your quality team signs off the workflow before it goes live."
  - q: "What does it cost?"
    a: "It's quoted per team size, and the three months is a fixed cost rather than an open-ended engagement, so you know the total before you start. Tell us how many people you'd send and we'll give you the number, along with the documentation we'd expect to compress first so you can weigh it against the hours."
  - q: "Our support workers aren't office-based. Will they use it?"
    a: "Most of the value lands on a phone, which is where they already are. The workflows start from a voice note or a few rough lines after a shift rather than assuming anyone sits at a desk — that's how this version of the program is built."
  - q: "Will this reduce support hours?"
    a: "It compresses documentation time, not support time. Providers come to us to stop unpaid after-hours writing and reduce the turnover that comes with it, so the hours saved go back into the roster and into workers finishing on time."
  - q: "Can it write in a participant's communication style?"
    a: "Yes, and it's one of the more valuable things it does — the same information at the reading level and in the format a particular participant or family can take in, including in another language. That's a real accessibility gain, and the worker still verifies before it goes out."
  - q: "We're a small provider with 15 staff. Is this viable?"
    a: "Documentation load per worker is usually higher in small providers, because there's no back office absorbing it. The program is the same three months regardless of team size."
